Goal:
The password for this level is ROT13 encoded and stored in a file.
The problem is that unlike base64 ROT13 is not a built-in function.

Approach:
A cheat or a simpler way to solve this is to just google ROT13 decoder.
But a cli approach would be to cat the contents and then decode.

Command:
cat data.txt | tr 'A-Za-z' 'N-ZA-Mn-za-m'

Breakdown:
the former part of the command pipe is used to extract the contents of the file.
The latter part of the command pipe is used to translate according to the ROT13 specifications.
applies a ROT13 cipher — each letter is shifted 13 places in the alphabet (A→N, B→O, ... N→A, and same pattern for lowercase).
The result is the decoded (or encoded) text printed to the terminal, since ROT13 is its own inverse.

Result:
We get the decoded password.

Key Takeaway:
This room introduced a new type of encoding to me and was fun to first use an online decoder,
but using the cli to solve this was tough but fun.
